WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.679 --> 00:00:05.170
Welcome everybody.

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:08.360
This is the second webinar for cloud products.

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:15.160
Today we will talk about using BigQuery to
explore and search the SRA metadata available.

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:23.530
Before we get started with the actual presentation,
I would like you to take a poll for us about

00:00:23.530 --> 00:00:28.640
your familiarity with SQL and BigQuery.

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:31.949
You can click on the screen to answer the
question.

00:00:31.949 --> 00:00:40.060
And when we get some responses, I will close
it and share it with everybody.

00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:44.480
This kind of helps me to tailor the webinar
to your needs.

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:50.110
So that I can go into more detail about certain
things or less detail.

00:00:50.110 --> 00:00:59.840
It looks like most of you have heard of SQL
but have not actually used it.

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:04.530
We have another poll for if you have used
BigQuery.

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:13.990
So besides helping tailor this to the webinar,
it helps us understand your use cases and

00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:17.700
the products we are building to meet your
needs.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:22.860
It looks like most of you have heard of or
used BigQuery.

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:27.649
For some of you, this is the first experience
you will have with it.

00:01:27.649 --> 00:01:31.290
I'm glad to be able to explain it.

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:34.381
So a quick introduction for the agenda.

00:01:34.381 --> 00:01:40.710
I will go through SRA in the Cloud and what
BigQuery is and after that short introduction,

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:46.300
I will jump straight into the actual demo
of the three use cases we will be doing today.

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:49.979
As far as the cloud, what is it?

00:01:49.979 --> 00:01:58.509
We have moved all of our data to the clouds
and more than 12 gigabytes to the cloud.

00:01:58.509 --> 00:02:01.180
This number grows daily.

00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:07.070
Having all the data doesn't help anybody because
you need a way of actually searching for the

00:02:07.070 --> 00:02:10.179
right data sets.

00:02:10.179 --> 00:02:16.579
This is where BigQuery comes and this is our
first cloud native product for doing search

00:02:16.579 --> 00:02:19.769
for data sets is.

00:02:19.769 --> 00:02:22.709
And it has two major benefits.

00:02:22.709 --> 00:02:28.749
One is it has a fast response to the queries
you will run and the multiple methods you

00:02:28.749 --> 00:02:29.909
can use to access it.

00:02:29.909 --> 00:02:33.400
It has a web interface, it has a command line
API.

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:40.290
It has multiple libraries you can use in the
programming languages like Python.

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:43.370
And also getting started with SQL is fairly
easy.

00:02:43.370 --> 00:02:48.120
There are only a handful of concepts you need
to understand before you can dive in and start

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:49.659
exploring it.

00:02:49.659 --> 00:02:57.859
If you are interested to learn more about
SQL, Google has made a lot of efforts to provide

00:02:57.859 --> 00:02:59.769
documentation for BigQuery.

00:02:59.769 --> 00:03:02.199
They have query syntax explanations.

00:03:02.199 --> 00:03:04.150
They have tutorials.

00:03:04.150 --> 00:03:07.400
They have how to documents.

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:12.219
Besides what Google provides, there are a
lot of tutorials and web classes you can sign

00:03:12.219 --> 00:03:13.219
up for.

00:03:13.219 --> 00:03:18.413
Some of them are free and some of them are
paid where you can learn SQL as well.

00:03:18.413 --> 00:03:21.959
Also search forums for questions on how to
write queries.

00:03:21.959 --> 00:03:26.799
One of the popular ones is [indiscernable].

00:03:26.799 --> 00:03:30.670
You can submit a question and people will
help you answer it.

00:03:30.670 --> 00:03:36.809
There are also a lot of questions being asked
already so you can certainly reuse those answers

00:03:36.809 --> 00:03:37.809
provided.

00:03:37.809 --> 00:03:41.969
And so BigQuery is just a giant data warehouse.

00:03:41.969 --> 00:03:46.099
They have deposited two datasets into it.

00:03:46.099 --> 00:03:53.469
The first is the SRA metadata that includes
sample information and how the sample is sequenced

00:03:53.469 --> 00:03:58.089
and basic statistics on the sequencing run.

00:03:58.089 --> 00:04:08.859
A second dataset we have is the SRA taxonomic
content analysis done using a kmer approach

00:04:08.859 --> 00:04:16.959
and we predict species based on these unique
kmers.

00:04:16.959 --> 00:04:23.099
And you can take results from both of the
data sets and combine them in a query.

00:04:23.099 --> 00:04:28.819
This will help you have a more filtered result
depending on the case.

00:04:28.819 --> 00:04:32.900
So a quick overview about SQL.

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:38.890
There is a SELECT statement that tells we
will be extracting data from tables.

00:04:38.890 --> 00:04:46.590
There is the FROM clause that will tell the
database, from what tables we will be extracting

00:04:46.590 --> 00:04:48.180
data from.

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:53.639
There is the WHERE clause that specifies the
filters we are applying to the tables.

00:04:53.639 --> 00:04:59.479
And a common one to use as use as ORDER BY,
which helps to order the dataset based on

00:04:59.479 --> 00:05:01.650
a particular column.

00:05:01.650 --> 00:05:06.610
It could be release date or accession numbers.

00:05:06.610 --> 00:05:11.080
There are other operators using SQL.

00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:13.110
Some of these are the =-equals-sign.

00:05:13.110 --> 00:05:21.790
Others you will see in my queries are the
In operator used to provide a list to a particular

00:05:21.790 --> 00:05:22.790
column.

00:05:22.790 --> 00:05:31.189
There is the UNnest which flattens an array
into its own table and the Like operator which

00:05:31.189 --> 00:05:34.039
allows you to do patterns searches.

00:05:34.039 --> 00:05:37.889
You will see those in my cases.

00:05:37.889 --> 00:05:41.789
We have three cases we will demo.

00:05:41.789 --> 00:05:46.880
One is finding the taxonomic distribution
of human gut meta- genomes.

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:56.030
The second is finding coronaviridae data in
SRA and the third is identifying runs for

00:05:56.030 --> 00:05:57.940
Arabidopsis gene expression studies.

00:05:57.940 --> 00:06:03.530
At this point, I will jump straight into the
actual demo.

00:06:03.530 --> 00:06:11.180
Here we have my first query which is finding
taxonomic content in human gut metagenomes.

00:06:11.180 --> 00:06:15.800
This takes about 30 seconds to run.

00:06:15.800 --> 00:06:18.210
To go over the syntax here.

00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:20.770
I'm selecting only three columns.

00:06:20.770 --> 00:06:27.840
I'm selecting the accession column, the name
column and a column called self_count which

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:32.889
represents the number of kmer hits.

00:06:32.889 --> 00:06:36.419
In the FROM clause I have two tables.

00:06:36.419 --> 00:06:38.229
One is the SRA metadata,
'nih-sra-datastore.sra.metadata',

00:06:38.229 --> 00:06:42.169
where I'm providing the alias m.

00:06:42.169 --> 00:06:46.300
And I have the SRA tax analysis tool table,
'nih-sra-datastore.sra_tax_analysis_tool.tax_analysis'

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:49.310
and the alias for that is tax.

00:06:49.310 --> 00:06:52.099
So the alias is just to make it easier to
write queries.

00:06:52.099 --> 00:06:56.340
And you don't have to rewrite the entire tablename
every time.

00:06:56.340 --> 00:07:01.279
I am then joining the two tables based on
accession columns.

00:07:01.279 --> 00:07:11.460
I am providing a list of organisms to search
in the metadata table for the organism column.

00:07:11.460 --> 00:07:13.849
And I'm looking for only public data sets.

00:07:13.849 --> 00:07:20.900
These data sets are publicly available and
don't require any permission to access.

00:07:20.900 --> 00:07:23.560
BigQuery does have dbGaP data in it.

00:07:23.560 --> 00:07:29.879
And so in order to make sure I don't need
to apply for special access to the studies,

00:07:29.879 --> 00:07:32.689
I'm just looking for public data.

00:07:32.689 --> 00:07:38.240
And filtering the results and providing only
the results for the species.

00:07:38.240 --> 00:07:48.039
The taxonomic analysis table contains the
entire taxonomic tree for each species identified.

00:07:48.039 --> 00:07:52.860
And if you are interested in seeing the whole
tree, you can certainly pull that out.

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:59.870
For this example, I just want the actual species
living in the human gut metagenome.

00:07:59.870 --> 00:08:07.580
So a couple things you can pay attention to
on the web interface is, they identify how

00:08:07.580 --> 00:08:13.110
much data will be processed with a query here
on the right-hand side.

00:08:13.110 --> 00:08:18.550
And it provides a quick check that there are
no syntax errors in the query.

00:08:18.550 --> 00:08:24.219
So this is kind of a quick check you can do
to make sure the query is fine.

00:08:24.219 --> 00:08:32.599
Now that we have our results, we have our
accessions column, the name for the species

00:08:32.599 --> 00:08:41.530
within this accession, and the number of kmer
hits.

00:08:41.530 --> 00:08:47.649
Looking at this, we can see the result came
back with roughly 39 million rows.

00:08:47.649 --> 00:08:51.500
This is a lot of data here.

00:08:51.500 --> 00:08:55.010
You can quickly scan through by eye if you
want.

00:08:55.010 --> 00:08:59.770
But we don't want to scan through 40 million
rows.

00:08:59.770 --> 00:09:04.480
So the next step might be, how do you visualize
something like this?

00:09:04.480 --> 00:09:10.730
What do you do to visualize in an easy manner?

00:09:10.730 --> 00:09:15.170
There are a couple of ways ways you can do
this.

00:09:15.170 --> 00:09:20.100
The first step would be to save the results
somewhere.

00:09:20.100 --> 00:09:27.260
Unfortunately, Google does provide certain
limits to what you can save to a file directly

00:09:27.260 --> 00:09:29.430
from running a query.

00:09:29.430 --> 00:09:36.991
In this case, there is the one gigabyte limit
or if you are saving to a local file, you

00:09:36.991 --> 00:09:38.750
can save 16,000 rows.

00:09:38.750 --> 00:09:43.971
However, there is an option to save the results
as its own BigQuery table and this is what

00:09:43.971 --> 00:09:45.480
you will actually need to do.

00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:53.130
Once you have saved it as the BigQuery table,
you have a couple of different options.

00:09:53.130 --> 00:09:59.209
You can send the the entire table to Google's
Data Studio.

00:09:59.209 --> 00:10:05.209
And use that to visualize the data and do
graphs and dashboards for data.

00:10:05.209 --> 00:10:12.350
Or if you want to run your own statistical
analysis on the data you can save the entire

00:10:12.350 --> 00:10:16.260
table as a file.

00:10:16.260 --> 00:10:22.089
This is why you need to save the results as
a table so you can send an export command

00:10:22.089 --> 00:10:29.560
either through the web interface command line
or through one of those libraries I mentioned

00:10:29.560 --> 00:10:37.149
for programming languages for exporting the
entire table into a file, into a Google bucket.

00:10:37.149 --> 00:10:42.630
When the export is finished, you have a file
which you can work on.

00:10:42.630 --> 00:10:47.820
However, if you do want to get that file to
your local computer, you would need to pay

00:10:47.820 --> 00:10:53.760
egress charges to download the data from your
Google bucket into your local computer.

00:10:53.760 --> 00:10:59.560
Depending on the use case and where you want
to perform the analysis, those are some of

00:10:59.560 --> 00:11:03.889
the different options available for you to
for you to work with such a large dataset

00:11:03.889 --> 00:11:05.170
as this.

00:11:05.170 --> 00:11:11.480
However, this can provide you some input about
what species live in human gut metagenomes

00:11:11.480 --> 00:11:15.790
so I think this is very useful.

00:11:15.790 --> 00:11:23.440
So now we will move forward to the second
use case.

00:11:23.440 --> 00:11:31.810
And you can see that I saved all of my use
cases as queries which is easy to do.

00:11:31.810 --> 00:11:40.639
You can click save query and save as and provide
any name you want to it.

00:11:40.639 --> 00:11:49.360
So for our second use case, finding coronaviridae
dataset, let me just open the query anrd run

00:11:49.360 --> 00:11:51.210
it very quickly.

00:11:51.210 --> 00:11:57.640
This is a much much smaller dataset that we
are querying, so comes back significantly

00:11:57.640 --> 00:11:58.640
faster.

00:11:58.640 --> 00:12:05.610
In this case, what I'm looking for is the
bioproject, the biosample accessions, the

00:12:05.610 --> 00:12:12.339
run accessions, the collection date, the geo
location of where the sample was collected,

00:12:12.339 --> 00:12:19.279
who it was collected by, and the host of the
sample.

00:12:19.279 --> 00:12:24.410
Just like last time, I'm querying the metadata
table and the taxonomy analysis table.

00:12:24.410 --> 00:12:30.040
What I'm searching for in this case is the
Coronaviridae family.

00:12:30.040 --> 00:12:36.970
Instead of looking for a particular species
or all species, I'm applying to a particular

00:12:36.970 --> 00:12:39.920
family that I'm looking for.

00:12:39.920 --> 00:12:46.649
As you can see, we have a lot of good metadata
like inn these first examples where we have

00:12:46.649 --> 00:12:51.730
a collection date, the location and who was
collected by, and the host.

00:12:51.730 --> 00:12:56.120
As you can see, there are data sets that don't
have a lot have a lot of metadata.

00:12:56.120 --> 00:13:01.970
It is one of those challenges with working
with user submitted data.

00:13:01.970 --> 00:13:07.300
What you might expect to be there might not
be there because it was never submitted to

00:13:07.300 --> 00:13:09.040
NCBI.

00:13:09.040 --> 00:13:16.850
Or in this case to EBI, since this is an ENA
accession.

00:13:16.850 --> 00:13:26.899
However, you can use these datasets to identify
runs that will be useful for the analysis.

00:13:26.899 --> 00:13:37.949
In case you are interested in the human based
hosts you can pull out the accessions, save

00:13:37.949 --> 00:13:47.819
those to a file, and feed those to the SRA
toolkit to dump fastq files or sam files and

00:13:47.819 --> 00:13:56.029
then run your computational tools on those
files.

00:13:56.029 --> 00:14:04.959
To make things easier within BigQuery, which
provides a lot of useful tools and shortcuts.

00:14:04.959 --> 00:14:07.029
One of these is an autocomplete feature.

00:14:07.029 --> 00:14:18.329
If I started writing something, and in this
case, I want to filter the dataset based on

00:14:18.329 --> 00:14:20.250
when the data sets were released.

00:14:20.250 --> 00:14:27.380
I'm going to search the metadata table, and
I just hit tab.

00:14:27.380 --> 00:14:31.139
And I could see all the columns within the
metadata table.

00:14:31.139 --> 00:14:38.629
And if I start just typing what I want, which
is the release date, it will select the date

00:14:38.629 --> 00:14:39.889
for me.

00:14:39.889 --> 00:14:48.220
In this case, I can find the column that I
want using this autocomplete feature.

00:14:48.220 --> 00:14:56.019
If you want to learn more about shortcuts,
there is this shortcut tool that you can look

00:14:56.019 --> 00:15:01.449
through and see different shortcuts available
for you.

00:15:01.449 --> 00:15:09.560
In this case, if I wanted to filter by release
date, I could say I want all the samples that

00:15:09.560 --> 00:15:14.410
were released within this year.

00:15:14.410 --> 00:15:19.199
And so it makes it very easy to write your
queries using this feature.

00:15:19.199 --> 00:15:28.440
You don't have to memorize all the columns
available in all of the tables.

00:15:28.440 --> 00:15:41.440
So the last example here is finding the arabidopsis
dataset for RNA-seq analysis.

00:15:41.440 --> 00:15:48.290
In this case, this query is a little more
complicated.

00:15:48.290 --> 00:15:54.990
Because I'm working with a lot more data submitted
by users.

00:15:54.990 --> 00:16:02.199
In this case, what I'm what I'm doing is,
I'm opening the attributes column within the

00:16:02.199 --> 00:16:03.430
metadata table.

00:16:03.430 --> 00:16:12.250
And this column is an array so I use the unnest
operator to make it a flat table.

00:16:12.250 --> 00:16:21.029
And I'm looking for all the keys with the
name, tissue, organism_part or organ_sample.

00:16:21.029 --> 00:16:29.070
And I'm providing an alias called tissue,
so I can quickly identify where the tissue

00:16:29.070 --> 00:16:31.649
was for the dataset.

00:16:31.649 --> 00:16:37.180
Next, I'm looking for what the treatment was
for the dataset.

00:16:37.180 --> 00:16:43.389
I basically copied and pasted the above query
and changed the keys to treatment, environment_stress

00:16:43.389 --> 00:16:46.370
and condition for this sample.

00:16:46.370 --> 00:16:54.339
So how do I know what keys to use in this
column?

00:16:54.339 --> 00:17:01.750
That was a more complicated step where I looked
through different examples of different samples.

00:17:01.750 --> 00:17:08.540
And I printed these out and saved them as
a list to be used in this query.

00:17:08.540 --> 00:17:15.880
There will be work trying to identify the
correct keys to use because different submitters

00:17:15.880 --> 00:17:20.740
like to provide different attribute names
when they are submitting metadata.

00:17:20.740 --> 00:17:25.170
It makes it hard to standardize searches like
this.

00:17:25.170 --> 00:17:32.610
And so besides that I'm also going to pull
out all the metadata columns, but I'm providing

00:17:32.610 --> 00:17:35.250
the EXCEPT operator here.

00:17:35.250 --> 00:17:39.870
Because I don't want these five columns to
be displaced.

00:17:39.870 --> 00:17:47.450
Instead of typing out the metadata accessions,
assay type, and all the other columns I want

00:17:47.450 --> 00:17:48.450
to display.

00:17:48.450 --> 00:17:52.980
It is quicker to supply a list of columns
I don't want to display.

00:17:52.980 --> 00:17:58.660
For the FROM clause, I am providing the metadata
table with the alias.

00:17:58.660 --> 00:18:06.100
And I'm going to UNnest again the attributes
array as its own table.

00:18:06.100 --> 00:18:11.080
BigQuery will join these these two tables
on the back end so you don't have to worry

00:18:11.080 --> 00:18:13.010
about them.

00:18:13.010 --> 00:18:20.070
The filters we are applying here is the actual
organism name and I will search through the

00:18:20.070 --> 00:18:27.410
simple attributes looking for the key words
either leaf, root or leaves.

00:18:27.410 --> 00:18:36.580
And then the other approach I took is the
metadata has this column called JATTR.

00:18:36.580 --> 00:18:42.520
And this is the json attributes column where
we took all the virus sample attributes and

00:18:42.520 --> 00:18:46.210
provided them as json.

00:18:46.210 --> 00:18:51.360
Here I am searching based on a pattern for
the word drought.

00:18:51.360 --> 00:19:00.330
The % sign will apply one or more characters
before or after the word drought.

00:19:00.330 --> 00:19:02.660
I'm doing abroad search here.

00:19:02.660 --> 00:19:14.240
And to make sure I'm pulling out the gene
expression ones, I provided the librarysource

00:19:14.240 --> 00:19:15.240
transcriptomic.

00:19:15.240 --> 00:19:16.700
And the assay_type is set to RNA-Seq.

00:19:16.700 --> 00:19:19.960
And this could potentially be another filter
you apply.

00:19:19.960 --> 00:19:29.290
Looking at the results we have roughly 300
datasets where the tissue is either leaf or

00:19:29.290 --> 00:19:34.690
root and where the treatment relates to drought.

00:19:34.690 --> 00:19:40.310
Having looked at this and maybe picking out
some other attributes to filter the dataset,

00:19:40.310 --> 00:19:48.960
maybe by sequencing platform or what selection
strategy was used, I can narrow the dataset

00:19:48.960 --> 00:19:49.960
even further.

00:19:49.960 --> 00:19:58.760
Now that you have this, the idea is you pull
out the run accessioins and get the actual

00:19:58.760 --> 00:20:05.420
data to run some bioinformatics RNA-Seq computation
on it.

00:20:05.420 --> 00:20:10.220
So those are the three use cases I wanted
to provide to you.

00:20:10.220 --> 00:20:17.690
Go through the queries and use them and potentially
use the results.

00:20:17.690 --> 00:20:23.540
Now, running BigQuery is not free but it is
very cheap.

00:20:23.540 --> 00:20:27.880
So here is a quick predicted cost for running
these queries.

00:20:27.880 --> 00:20:34.990
As you can see our biggest one was the human
gut meta- genome where we extracted 40 million

00:20:34.990 --> 00:20:38.500
rows and that only costs roughly 40 cents.

00:20:38.500 --> 00:20:42.650
The Coronaviridae dataset was significantly
smaller and was only 30 cents.

00:20:42.650 --> 00:20:48.410
And the ARABIDOPSIS was only 10 cents.

00:20:48.410 --> 00:20:52.620
Getting started with BigQuery is very cheap.

00:20:52.620 --> 00:20:56.240
We are always looking for feedback.

00:20:56.240 --> 00:21:01.630
The only way we know how to improve our tools
and to know what we are doing is if the community

00:21:01.630 --> 00:21:03.070
speaks to us.

00:21:03.070 --> 00:21:08.300
If you have suggestions for improvement, do
not hesitate to contact us.

00:21:08.300 --> 00:21:12.961
Just write to, suggest@ncbi.nlm.nih.gov and
provide us with any feedback on things that

00:21:12.961 --> 00:21:20.810
we are doing well or things he could improve
to fit your use cases.

00:21:20.810 --> 00:21:27.100
The more we hear from you, the better we can
provide the tools you need for research.

00:21:27.100 --> 00:21:34.050
Here are some general URLs that you can look
for, for more information.

00:21:34.050 --> 00:21:42.240
We have more documentation available about
BigQuery and access to data in the cloud at

00:21:42.240 --> 00:21:43.480
SRA.

00:21:43.480 --> 00:21:49.290
If you want to learn more about exporting
large datasets, Google does provide directions

00:21:49.290 --> 00:21:50.290
for that.

00:21:50.290 --> 00:21:55.890
If you want to play around with Data Studio
there is documentation available for that.

00:21:55.890 --> 00:22:01.980
And please sigh up for our YouTube channel
as we will hold more webinars and provide

00:22:01.980 --> 00:22:03.780
more instructional videos.

00:22:03.780 --> 00:22:06.780
All videos will be available there.

00:22:06.780 --> 00:22:11.600
So it is a good resource to have for you.

00:22:11.600 --> 00:22:13.450
And that is about it.

00:22:13.450 --> 00:22:14.450
Thank you.

00:22:14.450 --> 00:22:16.190
[Peter Cooper] Thanks Yuriy.

00:22:16.190 --> 00:22:18.870
We have a few questions and the questions
pod.

00:22:18.870 --> 00:22:22.360
There have been some questions about cost.

00:22:22.360 --> 00:22:26.010
You did a good job of explaining costs for
those particular queries.

00:22:26.010 --> 00:22:28.430
There is a free tier is there not?

00:22:28.430 --> 00:22:31.470
Can you run these on the free tier?

00:22:31.470 --> 00:22:38.470
[Yuriy Skripchenko] There Google does provide
kind of a free tier approach where the number

00:22:38.470 --> 00:22:43.050
of queries is limited but you could do that.

00:22:43.050 --> 00:22:51.660
And if you sign up for BigQuery yourself,
Google provides you one terabyte of data to

00:22:51.660 --> 00:22:54.410
process free every single month.

00:22:54.410 --> 00:23:00.070
It is possible to sign up and set a limit
for yourself for how much data you want to

00:23:00.070 --> 00:23:01.880
process.

00:23:01.880 --> 00:23:09.040
And just stay within that one terabyte free
limit which I think will be higher than the

00:23:09.040 --> 00:23:10.040
free tier.

00:23:10.040 --> 00:23:13.290
But that part I'm not quite sure about.

00:23:13.290 --> 00:23:16.330
There certainly is free tier for BigQuery.

00:23:16.330 --> 00:23:18.100
[Peter Cooper] Another question.

00:23:18.100 --> 00:23:22.020
Actually there are a couple related questions.

00:23:22.020 --> 00:23:28.010
And it has to do with the columns and the
keys that you search on.

00:23:28.010 --> 00:23:31.860
I think this was the Arabidopsis example.

00:23:31.860 --> 00:23:36.240
One question is, what happens if there are
multiple key matches?

00:23:36.240 --> 00:23:39.420
What results do you get back?

00:23:39.420 --> 00:23:44.660
For example, the information about treatment
could be three possible keys I think.

00:23:44.660 --> 00:23:46.390
[Yuriy Skripchenko] Right.

00:23:46.390 --> 00:23:48.470
It is certainly possible.

00:23:48.470 --> 00:23:53.560
In this case, there wasn't any overlap in
these three keys.

00:23:53.560 --> 00:23:56.800
[Peter Cooper] But it is possible there could
be?

00:23:56.800 --> 00:23:58.640
[Yuriy Skripchenko] Yes.

00:23:58.640 --> 00:24:01.720
Potentially, that that could be.

00:24:01.720 --> 00:24:07.580
I would actually need to explore that and
how this query would act that way.

00:24:07.580 --> 00:24:12.910
[Peter Cooper] I think it was a general question
as to how it would behave.

00:24:12.910 --> 00:24:15.490
[Yuriy Skripchenko] Yes.

00:24:15.490 --> 00:24:23.920
This is the complication with having user
submitted metadata where the keys can change

00:24:23.920 --> 00:24:29.380
and different submitters will use different
keys to mean the same thing.

00:24:29.380 --> 00:24:35.390
So you need to be very careful about how you
use those keys and you might potentially run

00:24:35.390 --> 00:24:41.890
into an error on the result depending how
the query is structured.

00:24:41.890 --> 00:24:47.630
So it does take some playing around with the
query to get the exact result that you want.

00:24:47.630 --> 00:24:50.110
[Peter Cooper] Thank you.

00:24:50.110 --> 00:24:56.220
Related to that is this concept of the user
submitted data having lack of control I guess

00:24:56.220 --> 00:24:58.710
is one way of putting it.

00:24:58.710 --> 00:25:04.790
Someone wanted to know if NCBI had done any
work to group related columns so that it would

00:25:04.790 --> 00:25:09.380
be easier for people to make a precise queries.

00:25:09.380 --> 00:25:11.650
[Yuriy Skripchenko] Right.

00:25:11.650 --> 00:25:17.670
So NCBI does try to standardize the metadata
that we request from people during submission

00:25:17.670 --> 00:25:20.960
time, that helps.

00:25:20.960 --> 00:25:29.151
We have not had the chance to do more work
on standardizing the metadata but it is certainly

00:25:29.151 --> 00:25:34.520
something that we are aware of that needs
to happen and we will be working on it in

00:25:34.520 --> 00:25:39.000
in the future to make this process easier
when writing queries.

00:25:39.000 --> 00:25:46.040
At this time, we have not dug too deep into
it simply because of the complexity of the

00:25:46.040 --> 00:25:49.820
task but it is something on the roadmap.

00:25:49.820 --> 00:25:53.800
[Peter Cooper] Thank you.

00:25:53.800 --> 00:25:58.420
Another question is about, is there a way
to run a query.

00:25:58.420 --> 00:26:04.690
Suppose you ran a query today and you want
want to run a query a month later but you

00:26:04.690 --> 00:26:08.130
want to only query the new data.

00:26:08.130 --> 00:26:11.360
The stuff that has come and in the past month
to save money basically.

00:26:11.360 --> 00:26:16.150
Is there a way to there a way to do that?

00:26:16.150 --> 00:26:22.360
[Yuriy Skripchenko] Not yet as far as I understand
how BigQuery works.

00:26:22.360 --> 00:26:27.590
It searches the entire dataset for results.

00:26:27.590 --> 00:26:36.490
You can certainly set filters for when the
data might have been submitted or released.

00:26:36.490 --> 00:26:39.060
So the return would be smaller.

00:26:39.060 --> 00:26:42.210
But BigQuery will still search the entire
dataset.

00:26:42.210 --> 00:26:45.740
[Peter Cooper] I see, so that would be applied
after the search.

00:26:45.740 --> 00:26:47.270
[Yuriy Skripchenko] Yes.

00:26:47.270 --> 00:26:48.800
[Peter Cooper] Okay.

00:26:48.800 --> 00:26:54.660
Another question which has to do with diagramming
the database.

00:26:54.660 --> 00:26:58.510
Is there a UML or ER model of the SRA tables?

00:26:58.510 --> 00:27:04.951
[Yuriy Skripchenko] We provide schemas in
the documentation that are available at SRA.

00:27:04.951 --> 00:27:10.170
So if we go to the SRA landing page.

00:27:10.170 --> 00:27:14.220
We have SRA in the cloud here.

00:27:14.220 --> 00:27:23.070
Within Search using BigQuery we have links
to the actual tables and some explanations

00:27:23.070 --> 00:27:25.240
of what columns can be used.

00:27:25.240 --> 00:27:30.410
We did not put together a full UML diagram.

00:27:30.410 --> 00:27:40.510
But you also can link the entire project,
if you go to Pin a project you would need

00:27:40.510 --> 00:27:46.500
to type in nih-sra-datastore.

00:27:46.500 --> 00:27:51.050
Then click PIN.

00:27:51.050 --> 00:27:55.900
And you would be able to take take a look
at what is available within BigQuery.

00:27:55.900 --> 00:28:00.520
You can take a look at the tables and see
what columns and types are available and what

00:28:00.520 --> 00:28:03.260
is available within BigQuery.

00:28:03.260 --> 00:28:07.010
Here you can take a look at the tables.

00:28:07.010 --> 00:28:14.540
And see what columns and types are available
for all of these data tables from within BigQuery.

00:28:14.540 --> 00:28:18.750
It is a good idea to build the UML diagrams.

00:28:18.750 --> 00:28:19.750
[Peter Cooper] Okay thanks.

00:28:19.750 --> 00:28:25.430
And there is another question that you may
have addressed in the last webinar.

00:28:25.430 --> 00:28:30.410
Someone is asking if we have a pipeline available
that they can submit data.

00:28:30.410 --> 00:28:35.160
It looks like they are trying to filter out
contamination from the data.

00:28:35.160 --> 00:28:38.390
[Yuriy Skripchenko] I don't quite understand
the question here.

00:28:38.390 --> 00:28:40.980
A Pipeline to submit data?

00:28:40.980 --> 00:28:41.980
The metadata?

00:28:41.980 --> 00:28:43.260
[Peter Cooper] I'm not sure.

00:28:43.260 --> 00:28:47.300
I think they were asking about contamination
of the data that they want to filter out.

00:28:47.300 --> 00:28:49.180
I'm not sure what kind of contamination.

00:28:49.180 --> 00:28:53.620
Maybe human sequences in metagenome data or
something like that.

00:28:53.620 --> 00:28:54.990
[Yuriy Skripchenko] Okay.

00:28:54.990 --> 00:29:02.150
We do have a process for filtering out human
data from datasets.

00:29:02.150 --> 00:29:09.440
Whether the dataset is submitted to NCBI,
if the submitter contacts SRA and tells us

00:29:09.440 --> 00:29:15.890
they want to perform human filtering on the
dataset we can activate the process.

00:29:15.890 --> 00:29:22.440
We have not built that into our submission
interface yet to allow the submitter to do

00:29:22.440 --> 00:29:23.780
that themselves.

00:29:23.780 --> 00:29:29.110
We can certainly do that and we have a process
for that.

00:29:29.110 --> 00:29:35.900
We are working on a publication for that process
at this moment and once the publication is

00:29:35.900 --> 00:29:39.680
made, we will be linking it to the webpages.

00:29:39.680 --> 00:29:42.090
[Peter Cooper] Great.

00:29:42.090 --> 00:29:43.700
Very helpful.

00:29:43.700 --> 00:29:49.470
I think we have addressed all the questions.

00:29:49.470 --> 00:29:50.610
Thank you everybody for coming.

00:29:50.610 --> 00:29:51.610
Thank you, Yuriy.

00:29:51.610 --> 00:29:53.360
[Yuriy Skripchenko] Thank you everybody for
joining us.

00:29:53.360 --> 00:29:56.490
[Peter Cooper] I will go ahead and end the
webinar.

